// ===========================================================================================
// COSMOS-SDK L1 EXPLOIT-PoC HARNESS — keeper/message test   [analog of a focused Foundry test]
// ---
// WHY THIS EXISTS
//   On a Cosmos L1 the static/symbolic backstop for the money classes is near-nil (no Slither/
//   Halmos; no linter catches the halt class — see docs/cosmos-notes.md §4). Recall therefore
//   rests on hand-written Go PoCs + disciplined state-machine review. This template is the spine
//   for the SINGLE-MODULE bug classes (theft, invariant break, authority bypass, ante gap): stand
//   up the module's keeper on an in-memory store, drive the ATTACKER message, and end on a
//   QUANTIFIED assertion — the same "focused test → assertion" shape as Foundry.
//
//   In-process (no running node), so a PoC runs in milliseconds. Use it first. Escalate to the
//   app-level determinism harness (app_determinism_test.go.template) for halt/nondeterminism
//   classes, or to localnet_repro.sh.template for a validator-observable halt — and record why in
//   the ledger row.
//
// CONTRACT (fill per target)
//   Precondition : the keeper + honest state are set up the way an integration deploy would
//                  (correct params, module account funded, a legit user's funds present).
//   Postcondition: the named theft/lock/break is demonstrated on CONCRETE state reads (attacker
//                  balance up / module account down / a legit user's funds now unwithdrawable /
//                  a registered invariant now reporting broken) — NOT a counter this harness wrote.
//   Invariant    : the quantified property that MUST hold (Σ balances conserved; only the correct
//                  authority mutates privileged state; supply == tracked). The PoC breaks exactly one.
//   Canary       : assert the ATTACK STATE WAS REACHED — the attacker msg actually executed and
//                  landed the deep state (err == nil where success is the exploit, or the exact
//                  predicted error) — before trusting any "held / REFUTED" result. A green test that
//                  never drove the vulnerable handler is a lie (repo rule).
//
// SETUP
//   1) Match the target's go.mod versions (cosmos-sdk / cometbft / cosmossdk.io/math) — the keeper
//      test setup helpers differ across SDK v0.47/v0.50/v0.53. Prefer the target module's OWN
//      test setup (most modules ship `keeper_test.go` / a `testutil` fixture) — reuse it, don't
//      reinvent the store/context wiring.
//   2) Copy this into the target module's package (e.g. x/mymodule/keeper/) so internal helpers
//      and the module's own fixtures are in scope.
//   3) Fill every TODO. Run:  go test ./x/<module>/... -run TestPoC -v
//
// NOTE: SDK keeper-test scaffolding drifts across versions (testutil.DefaultContextWithDB,
// keepertest fixtures, the depinject/app-wiring test helpers). If a helper below doesn't resolve,
// use the TARGET module's existing test setup rather than papering over the signature. Do NOT
// invent an API — pin to the target's SDK version and use its real one.
//
// SETUP REALITY (validated end-to-end on a real Cosmos keeper bug — engagement 13 H-01 tracer-bullet,
// 2026-07-24; the scaffold's shape held, these are the things it under-stated):
//   • The fixture may live IN the test package itself (`package keeper_test`, an in-file
//     `createDefaultTestInput(t)`), NOT a separate `testutil/keeper` package. So this PoC MUST be
//     `package <module>_test` and dropped INTO `x/<module>/keeper/` to reach unexported helpers
//     (deploy/mint/address helpers). Copy the repo to a WRITABLE dir first — never mutate a
//     read-only engagement checkout; add your test there.
//   • Deps are often HAND-WIRED (MountStoreWithDB + manual keeper construction), not depinject,
//     and collaborators may be MOCKS the fixture ships (e.g. a MockCommunityPoolKeeper). Read what
//     the mock RECORDS — asserting on a mocked collaborator's recorded state is a first-class,
//     faithful post-state (see the mock-assertion variant in PoC 1).
//   • A bug can live in an ARGUMENT the module passes to a collaborator, not in a bank-balance
//     delta. If so, assert on what the module PASSED (the mock's tally), not only on GetBalance.
//   • Reaching the vulnerable branch may need the module's OWN tx/EVM helpers to build
//     preconditions (e.g. real `evm/0x..` denoms via deployERC20/mintERC20 before BurnCoins).
//   • AMPLIFY so a loop bug shows as a NUMBER: if a per-item loop misuses the whole set, drive it
//     with >=2 qualifying items so the N x over-move is an unmissable quantified break (1 item can
//     hide it — the module's own existing test missed H-01 for exactly this reason).
//   • CONFIRM THE ASSERTION DISCRIMINATES: flip the one-line fix, re-run, see the test FAIL, then
//     revert. A PoC whose assertion passes on patched code proves nothing (PP Tip 37).
// ===========================================================================================

